tmux-flow
=========

A custom fork of tmux that tracks upstream (https://github.com/tmux/tmux)
and layers in opinionated defaults for a modern terminal workflow:

  - F-key bindings with no prefix required (phone-friendly)
  - Mouse support on by default
  - Tabs show current directory name instead of hostname
  - `tmux` with no args attaches to existing session or creates one
  - F12 session/directory chooser via sesh + fzf
  - F1 shows full key binding reference
  - Built-in WakaTime/Wakapi time tracking (fires on pane focus)

Opt-in, by sourcing tmux-flow.conf (see Install):

  - Catppuccin theme with automatic macOS dark/light switching
  - Session save/restore via tmux-resurrect (survives restarts)
  - Auto-save every 15 min via tmux-continuum

Install
-------

  $ brew install drpedapati/tools/tmux-flow

This installs tmux-flow plus the tools the F12 chooser needs:
sesh, fzf and zoxide.

Then start tmux:

  $ tmux

The key bindings, mouse support and status bar below are compiled into
the binary and need no configuration at all.

The Catppuccin theme and session save/restore ship with tmux-flow but
are opt-in, because turning them on means loading plugins into your
config. To enable them, add one line to ~/.tmux.conf:

  source $(brew --prefix tmux-flow)/share/tmux-flow/tmux-flow.conf

`brew info tmux-flow` prints the exact path for your install.

Key Bindings (no prefix required)
-----------------------------------

  F1            Key binding reference (this list, inside tmux)
  F2            New window
  F3 / F4       Previous / next window
  F5            Split left/right
  F6            Detach
  F7            Copy mode
  F8            Rename window
  F9            Split top/bottom
  F10           Other pane
  F11           Kill pane
  F12           Session/directory chooser (sesh + fzf)

  Shift+F3/F4   Previous / next pane
  Ctrl+F3/F4    Swap pane backward / forward
  Ctrl+F6       Kill pane
  Ctrl+F8       Rename session
  Shift+F8      Cycle layout
  Shift+F11     Zoom pane
  Alt+F11       Break pane to new window

  M-Left/Right  Previous / next window
  M-Up/Down     Previous / next session
  Shift+Arrows  Select pane by direction

Session Backup (resurrect + continuum)
-----------------------------------

Once you have sourced tmux-flow.conf (see Install), sessions are saved
automatically every 15 minutes.

  prefix + Ctrl-s   Save session now
  prefix + Ctrl-r   Restore last saved session

The plugins ship inside the Homebrew prefix, so there is nothing to
clone. Saves go to ~/.local/share/tmux/resurrect/ by default and survive
kill-server, reboots and brew upgrades.

Time Tracking (Wakapi)
-----------------------------------

tmux-flow fires a heartbeat every time you switch panes, tracking
time automatically by project directory. Sends to wakapi.dev — a
free, open-source WakaTime-compatible backend. Works across all your
machines with one account.

Setup:

  1. Create an account at https://wakapi.dev and copy your API key.

  2. Create ~/.wakatime.cfg:

       [settings]
       api_key = YOUR_KEY_HERE
       api_url = https://wakapi.dev/api

  2. Start tmux — tracking begins automatically.

  No ~/.wakatime.cfg or no api_key = silent no-op, zero overhead.

Changelog
-----------------------------------

v2.1 — 2026-08-08
  Stopped forcing @continuum-restore on. It made continuum restore a
  saved session about a second after every server start, with no user
  action, racing `new-session -A -s main` and sometimes failing with
  "duplicate session: main". Set it yourself if you want that behaviour:

    set -g @continuum-restore 'on'

  Bug reports and pull requests now route to this fork rather than to
  the upstream tmux mailing list.

  Homebrew bottles: installing no longer compiles tmux from source.

v2.0 — 2026-08-08
  Merged upstream's tmux 3.8 development line (731 commits). Brings
  floating panes, modal panes, upstream's own light/dark theme system,
  the C-b g move table and the Tab/BTab pane switcher.

  New prefix bindings kept from upstream, none of which have an F-key
  equivalent here:
    @      toggle pane between floating and tiled
    T      change the pane title
    Tab    switch to a window
    BTab   switch to a session

  Bindings this fork had already retired stay retired, including
  upstream's new floating-aware M-arrow and C-arrow resize variants.

  Fixed a macOS build failure: tmux 3.8 makes the jemalloc choice
  mandatory on Darwin, so the Homebrew formula now passes
  --enable-jemalloc and depends on jemalloc. Without it `brew install`
  aborted at configure on macOS.

  `tmux -V` now reports next-3.8, an upstream development snapshot
  rather than a tagged release. Note that 19 default styles (copy-mode
  selection and search highlighting among them) now render from
  upstream's built-in theme palette rather than Catppuccin, because
  switch-theme.sh overrides 5 of the 24 theme-aware options.

v1.9 — 2026-08-08
  Reworked the Homebrew formula. The old post_install cloned plugins
  into ~/.tmux/plugins and copied helper scripts there; it never worked,
  because Homebrew runs post_install with HOME pointed at a sandboxed
  temporary directory. The Catppuccin theme and session save/restore had
  therefore never worked for any install.

  Plugins now ship as pinned, checksummed resources inside the Homebrew
  prefix, and nothing is written to your home directory. Enable them by
  sourcing tmux-flow.conf (see Install).

  Dropped the wakatime-cli dependency; the heartbeat has used curl since
  v1.4 because wakatime-cli rejects Wakapi keys.

v1.8 — 2026-08-08
  Merged upstream tmux 3.7b (326 commits), moving off the next-3.7
  development line onto a released tag. Added the `*` new-pane binding.
  Release tooling now works on Linux as well as macOS.

v1.7 — 2026-07-16
  Release tooling hardening; releases publish reviewed commits only.

v1.6 — 2026-04-17
  Fixed a 100% CPU regression caused by `#{}` in style strings.

v1.5, v1.4 — 2026-04-17
  Catppuccin Latte status bar fixes; Option+i/o to move windows;
  Wakapi heartbeat field mapping.

v1.3 — 2026-03-27
  F12 chooser opens directories in the main session.

v1.0 - v1.2 — 2026-03-23
  First tagged releases as tmux-flow: F-key bindings, mouse on by
  default, Catppuccin theme, sesh/fzf session chooser, resurrect and
  continuum session backup, Wakapi time tracking.

--------

Welcome to tmux!

tmux is a terminal multiplexer: it enables a number of terminals to be created,
accessed, and controlled from a single screen. tmux may be detached from a
screen and continue running in the background, then later reattached.

This release runs on OpenBSD, FreeBSD, NetBSD, Linux, macOS and Solaris.

* Dependencies

tmux depends on libevent 2.x, available from:

	https://github.com/libevent/libevent/releases/latest

It also depends on ncurses, available from:

	https://invisible-mirror.net/archives/ncurses/

To build tmux, a C compiler (for example gcc or clang), make, pkg-config and a
suitable yacc (yacc or bison) are needed.

* Installation

To build and install tmux from a release tarball, use:

	$ ./configure && make
	$ sudo make install

tmux can use the utempter library to update utmp(5), if it is installed - run
configure with --enable-utempter to enable this.

To get and build the latest from version control - note that this requires
autoconf, automake and pkg-config:

	$ git clone https://github.com/drpedapati/tmux-flow.git
	$ cd tmux
	$ sh autogen.sh
	$ ./configure && make
	$ sudo make install

* Contributing

Bug reports, feature suggestions and especially code contributions are most
welcome. Please send by email to:

	tmux-users@googlegroups.com

Or open a GitHub issue or pull request.

* Documentation

For documentation on using tmux, see the tmux.1 manpage. View it from the
source tree with:

	$ nroff -mdoc tmux.1|less

A small example configuration is in example_tmux.conf.

Other documentation is available in the wiki:

	https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki

Also see the tmux FAQ at:

	https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/FAQ

A bash(1) completion file is at:

	https://github.com/scop/bash-completion/blob/main/completions/tmux

For debugging, run tmux with -v and -vv to generate server and client log files
in the current directory.

* Support

The tmux mailing list for general discussion and bug reports is:

	https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/tmux-users

Subscribe by sending an email to:

	tmux-users+subscribe@googlegroups.com

